%rec: Binding
%doc: How one volume is stored on its medium: the container (filesystem) and the
+ protection (error correction) it was made with. These change with the medium;
+ the files and the rest of the catalogue do not (research/plan.md, four layers).
+ Container: iso9660+udf-1.02, udf-2.50 (later perhaps ltfs, exfat, tar, afs).
+ Protection: rs03 or none. Media, Filesystem and Ecc describe them for people
+ (format 0.2 and earlier kept those three in the Disc record).
%key: Volume
%mandatory: Volume Container Protection
%type: MediumSectors int

Volume: PHOTO-01_1974-2021_F
Container: udf-2.50
Protection: none
Media: M-DISC BD-R 25GB
Filesystem: UDF 2.50, BD-ROM layout with metadata partition and a real mirror (arv udfwrite)
Ecc: none

%rec: Object
%doc: A data object: one file or folder put on a disc as a whole (a disc plan's item, or a
+ folder arv make puts on one disc), with no .arv marker of its own. Uuid is its lineage:
+ every version of the same object shares it.
+ Tree is the SHA-256 of its manifest (each file's SHA-256 and path within the object,
+ sorted; a repository's .git aside, as git.tsv describes it); Version counts its versions,
+ and the same Tree on another disc is the same version. Disc and Path say where it is
+ (Path under data/; "." for the whole of data/). Source is where it was read from: it is
+ kept in the home catalogue only, and discs carry their objects without it. Except (one
+ for each) names a file or folder of the source left out of the object because a disc plan
+ placed it elsewhere (a folder split): the object is the folder without them. Arranged, on a disc with
+ a Layout, is where it was arranged (its path in the disc plan) before the layout placed it
+ at Path; Name keeps its name where it was read from, and Slug, when they differ, is its name
+ on this disc. SameBytes (UUID VERSION) names an
+ object of another lineage with the same Tree: identity is the lineage, never the bytes,
+ so the same bytes read from elsewhere are another object, and this says so; a person who
+ knows they are one says so with an event (Object: object:THIS, SameAs: object:OTHER), and
+ readers then read its records as the other's versions with the same Tree.
+ Packed, it is one file on the disc: Pack names that file under data/ (the object's own, next to
+ its Path, or the disc's data/pack.*), and Packing says what it is (tar, tar+zstd, tar+xz,
+ tar+gzip, tar+lzip, tar+bzip2, zip; for a file also zstd, xz, gzip, lzip, bzip2). Unpacked where
+ it is, a pack puts each object it holds at its Path; Tree, Files and Bytes are those of its files.
+ Without Pack, Packing says the file is itself an archive (7z too), as it was given.
+ Title, Description, Coverage (EDTF), Rights and Origin (where it was published, as host/path)
+ say what it is: read from its own files when it is archived (its README, package manifest,
+ licence, a repository's commits and origin remote), or written by a person or a model
+ (arv describe NAME), with Notes.
%mandatory: Uuid Version Tree Disc Path Kind Date
%type: Uuid uuid
%type: Version int
%type: Files int
%type: Bytes int
%type: Date date
%type: Kind enum file folder git

