%rec: Event
%doc: Preservation actions and metadata changes, appended and never edited. Type uses
+ the PREMIS eventType vocabulary: https://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/preservation/eventType
+ Disc names the disc; a change to a location or a data object names it in Object
+ (location:CODE, object:UUID; name:KEY for an entry of the known names, kept at home) instead.
+ Agent (one or more) is software (arv@COMMIT), a model (llm:MODEL, embeddings:MODEL) or a person (human:LOGIN).
+ Authorship says how people and machines shared the work: automatic (software
+ following rules), suggested (a model, not reviewed), accepted (a model's suggestion
+ a person kept as it was), edited (one a person changed), human (a person alone).
+ A replication records one copy: Copy (its letter, A, B ... unique among the disc's copies and
+ written on the disc after its id), Form (disc, iso: the image as a file, folder: the disc's
+ files), Temperature (hot, warm, cold: given, else its place's, else cold for a disc and
+ warm for a file or folder), Location (where this copy is kept), Path for a file or folder,
+ MediaType (the media's maker and type, as the drive reports it) and Identifier when known:
+ how this physical copy is recognised, as scheme:value (bca: its factory serial, barcode: a
+ label stuck on and scanned, serial: a manufacturer's serial), repeatable, and added later by
+ any event that names the Copy. With ReadBack: identical
+ it is a copy read back against its image before it was recorded (known good), as is one a
+ later fixity check with its Copy passed. A disc's copies are its replications, counted as
+ physical discs: two that share a bca:, barcode: or serial: identifier are one disc recorded
+ twice. A stored copy may name its Carrier, the tape or drive holding it (and other discs'
+ copies): two copies of one disc on one carrier count as one.
+ Every event has an EventId: the first 32 hex digits of the SHA-256 of its other fields as
+ written (a Nonce keeps two alike apart). An event about one copy names it by Replication (that
+ copy's replication EventId) and Copy (its letter then; the newest is its letter now). A change
+ to a field says Sets: FIELD and Replaces: the EventIds of the changes it follows (a path's
+ tags or caption after burning: Tags: PATH<TAB>TAGS or Caption: PATH<TAB>TEXT, with Sets:
+ Tags:PATH or Caption:PATH; the disc's tags.tsv stays as burned; a data object's tags or
+ name now: an event about it, Object: object:UUID, with Tags: TAGS or Slug: NAME, Sets: Tags
+ or Slug); two that
+ replace the same one were made apart, a conflict to settle by setting the field again. Deaccession retires a disc (its Disc record gets
+ Retired).
%mandatory: Type Date Outcome
%type: Outcome enum success failure warning
%type: Authorship enum automatic suggested accepted edited human
%type: Disc rec Disc
%type: Location rec Location

EventId: ID
